# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL
@module graphics ; The graphics: the CPC mode-1 pictures of the play screen, the ball, the objects and the cells
@about Every picture here is Amstrad CPC mode-1 data: a byte holds 4 pixels in two planes (pen = bit 7-i + 2 x bit 3-i). The Spectrum's blitters (DrawSprite $E318, XorSprite $E3EE, the coloured $E3E5) show the low nibble only, one bit a pixel, so a CPC byte is 4 Spectrum pixels and a picture w bytes wide is 4w pixels; the bricks (XorBricks $E4C4) show the high nibble. Width and height are never stored: each caller sets SpriteWidth $A9FE and the rows in B. The resources are 4-pen PNGs (src/res/sprites/README.md).
@about The leftovers $61A8, $6758, $75A8 and $8385 are bytes no routine reads (no recording reads them either); they look like more CPC pictures of the original.
$61A8 b UnusedPicture61A8 16 ; Never read: 496 bytes of CPC mode-1 picture data left from the original (known only from the bytes)
> No instruction refers to $61A8-$6397 and no recording reads it (work/stage1/memmap-use.txt). The bytes are mostly $F0 and $0F runs, the shape of a CPC frame or panel. Kept as leftovers/unused-61a8.bin.
@res leftovers/unused-61a8.bin
$6398 b SidePanels 16 ; The two side panels, each 2 bytes x 192 lines, drawn by DrawSides $B488
> DrawSides $B488 draws SidePanels at the CPC address $F800 (left edge, B = $C0 lines, SpriteWidth 2) and SidePanelRight $6518 at $F83C (right edge) once per game start. Witness: read by every recording that starts a game (c1, menu, gameover, rzx).
@res sprites/sides.bin
$6518 SidePanelRight ; the right panel, 384 bytes
$6698 b ExitSign 16 ; The EXIT sign: two pictures of 8 bytes x 7 lines, read only by the unrun DrawExitSign $D08E
> DrawExitSign $D08E (never called) would XOR the first picture one character row above the exit's cell. No recording reads these 112 bytes. Known only from the code.
@res sprites/exit-sign.bin
$6708 b BlankPicture 16 ; 80 zero bytes: the blank picture that erases a cell, the ball's last burst frame and the exit's first frame
> Read by the burst's last step ($B1CB, 4 x 14), FillColumn ($B4E1), OpenExit ($BCE1, 8 x 10), the fire put-out ($C126) and the lock opening's end ($C4B8). Its bytes are all zero, so a stored blank clears and an XOR leaves the screen as it is.
$6758 s Unused6758 ; 144 zero bytes no routine reads
$67E8 b BallPictures 16 ; The ball: 16 pictures of 4 bytes x 10 lines for player 1, then 16 for player 2 at BallPictures2 $6A68
> BallPictures (the round ball) and the 15 rolling frames $6810-$6A40, 40 bytes apart, chosen by BallFramesRight $C4E2 / BallFramesLeft $C502 for each step of a move. Player 2's ball is the same set $280 bytes on ($ADE6 adds $0280 when CurrentPlayer $ADDB is 2).
> Witness: read every frame of play (the ball is drawn by XOR, erased and drawn again); BallPictures2 is read only in two-player turns of player 2 (work/stage2/rec/twoplayer).
@res sprites/ball.bin
$6A68 BallPictures2 ; player 2's ball, 16 pictures
$6CE8 b ObjectPictures 16 ; The objects 1-40: 4 bytes x 14 lines each (56 bytes), kind k at $6CB0 + 56k
> TakeObject $B958 and BuildLevelObject $CFF4 find kind k at $6CB0 + 56 x k (kind 0, no object, is never drawn, so $6CB0 is only a base inside BallPictures2). Drawn in the ink ObjectInks $C6F6[k] by $E3E5. Kinds: 1 hammer, 2 key, 3 water, 4 bottle (an extra life), 5-40 prizes (src/res/sprites/README.md).
@res sprites/objects.bin
$75A8 b UnusedPicture75A8 16 ; Never read: 480 bytes of CPC picture data left from the original (known only from the bytes)
@res leftovers/unused-75a8.bin
$7788 b Platform1Pictures 8 ; Platform type 1 (and the plain one under a fire, type 8): rest, two take-off and three spring pictures, 8 bytes wide
> Rest $7788 (4 lines), take-off $77A8 and $77F8 (10 lines each, also the conveyors 6 and 7, PlatDraw6/7), spring $7848 (7), $7880 (9), $78C8 (7): the pictures SpringPics $C5D6 and TakeoffPics $C64E name for type 1. Drawn by XOR at the cell's screen address CellScrAddr $AB2D.
@res sprites/platform-1.bin
$77A8 Platform1Takeoff ; take-off, 8 x 10; the conveyor left (type 6)
$77F8 Platform1Takeoff2 ; take-off, 8 x 10; the conveyor right (type 7)
$7848 Platform1Spring ; spring pictures: 8 x 7, 8 x 9, 8 x 7
$7900 b Platform2Pictures 8 ; Platform type 2 (crumbling, first stage): rest 8 x 4, take-off 8 x 10 twice, spring 8 x 7, 8 x 9, 8 x 7
@res sprites/platform-2.bin
$7A78 b Platform3Pictures 6 ; Platform type 3 (crumbling, second stage), 6 bytes wide: rest 6 x 4, take-off 6 x 8 twice, spring 6 x 6, 6 x 7, 6 x 6
@res sprites/platform-3.bin
$7B62 b Platform4Pictures 4 ; Platform type 4 (crumbling, last stage), 4 bytes wide: rest 4 x 4, take-off 4 x 6 twice, spring 4 x 5, 4 x 6, 4 x 5
@res sprites/platform-4.bin
$7BE2 b Platform5Pictures 8 ; Platform type 5 (the stopping platform, and the one under a fire, type 9): rest 8 x 4, take-off 8 x 10 twice, spring 8 x 7, 8 x 9, 8 x 7
@res sprites/platform-5.bin
$7D5A b SpikesPicture 6 ; Platform type 12, the spikes: 6 bytes x 7 lines (PlatDraw12 $D1B2)
@res sprites/spikes.bin
$7D84 b CrumblePictures 8 ; The crumbling platforms' falling pictures: 8 x 4, then three of 6 x 4 (CrumbleType3), three of 4 x 4, three of 2 x 4 and four of 2 x 5
> Drawn one a step by the crumble steps ($C258-$C3BF) at CrumbleAddr $AB24 as a platform wears from type 2 to 3, 3 to 4 and 4 to nothing.
@res sprites/crumble-8.bin
$7DA4 b CrumbleType3 6 ; Three falling pictures of 6 x 4
@res sprites/crumble-6.bin
$7DEC b CrumbleType4 4 ; Three falling pictures of 4 x 4
@res sprites/crumble-4.bin
$7E1C b CrumbleSmall 2 ; Three falling pictures of 2 x 4
@res sprites/crumble-2.bin
$7E34 b CrumbleBits 2 ; Four falling pictures of 2 x 5, the last pieces
@res sprites/crumble-bits.bin
$7E5C b WallPictures 16 ; The walls, 2 bytes x 24 lines each: wall 4's four frames, the locks 3, 2, 1, the flash, the lock's five opening frames
> Wall 4 (no key opens it) cycles $7E5C + 48n, n 0-3, every fourth frame (AnimateWalls $BC30). Locks: type 3 $7F1C, type 2 $7F4C, type 1 $7F7C (WallDraw3/2/1); $7FAC the flash of a key used; $7FDC-$809C the five frames of a type-1 lock opening ($C492-$C4B1). Drawn at WallScrAddr $AB2F.
@res sprites/walls.bin
$7F1C WallLock3 ; lock type 3
$7F4C WallLock2 ; lock type 2
$7F7C WallLock1 ; lock type 1
$7FAC WallFlash ; a lock taking a key
$7FDC WallOpening ; a type-1 lock opening, five frames
$80CC b ExitMark 9 ; The exit: a 1-byte x 9-line mark, then four frames of 6 bytes x 5 lines at ExitFrames $80D5
> OpenExit $BCB9 draws ExitMark ($BCF6, SpriteWidth 1, 9 lines); AnimateExit $BD21 cycles the frames from the table $C522.
@res sprites/exit-mark.bin
$80D5 b ExitFrames 6 ; The exit's four frames, 6 x 5
@res sprites/exit.bin
$814D b FirePictures 6 ; Flames: three frames of 6 bytes x 5 lines over a burning platform (types 8 and 9)
> AnimateFires $BBCD picks frame FireFrame $AA16 every fourth frame; PlatDraw8/9 draw the first at the build.
@res sprites/fire.bin
$81A7 b SteamPictures 6 ; The water drop's steam over a fire put out: 6 x 6, 6 x 3, 6 x 2, then 4 x 3 at SteamSmall $81E9
> Drawn by the fire put-out steps ($C13E-$C185) at FireOutAddr $AB29.
@res sprites/steam.bin
$81E9 b SteamSmall 4 ; the last steam picture, 4 x 3
@res sprites/steam-small.bin
$81F5 b BurstPictures 4 ; The ball bursting: six pictures of 4 bytes x 14 lines (state 6, $B195-$B1C2), then BlankPicture
@res sprites/burst.bin
$8345 b BrickPicture 8 ; One brick layer: 8 bytes x 8 lines, drawn by XorBricks $E4C4 from the high nibbles
> DrawBricks $D180 draws it once a layer still standing (BrickLayers $AAD7, 8 for a whole brick).
@res sprites/bricks.bin
$8385 b Unused8385 16 ; Never read: 2231 bytes left from the original (known only from the bytes)
> No instruction refers to $8385-$8C3B; no recording reads it. The bytes look like more CPC mode-1 pictures. Note that LevelRecords is reached as $8BF1 + 75 x (level + 1) (BuildLevel $CF7E), an address inside this block that is never read.
@res leftovers/unused-8385.bin
@module levels ; The level data, the cells and the level builder
@about The 100 levels are 75-byte records at LevelRecords $8C3C: 36 cells (a wall type in the high nibble, a platform type in the low one), the ball's start cell, the prizes to take, the cell the exit appears in, and an object for each cell. BuildLevel $CF2E copies the record of Level $AB00 to LevelRecord $AA20 and splits the cells into CellPlatforms $AA6B and CellWalls $AA8F. The text form is src/res/levels/levels.txt.
$8C3C b LevelRecords 75 ; The 100 level records, 75 bytes each (level 0 first)
> BuildLevel $CF2E reads record Level $AB00 (as $8BF1 + 75 x (Level + 1)) once per life start. Record: +0..+35 the cells, row by row from the top, 6 a row (wall << 4 | platform); +36 the start cell; +37 the prizes; +38 the exit's cell; +39..+74 the objects (0 none, 1-40).
> Witness: 88 levels read by the RZX recordings rzx l00-l99 (the record of each level they reach); the other levels are read by stage 2's level recordings (work/stage2/rec).
@res levels/levels.bin
$A988 s LevelsEnd ; 117 zero bytes after the last record, never read
@module vars ; The game's variables: the ball, the cells of the level, the player, the animations
@about The variables from $A9FD to $AB5E. Every value is written by the game before it is read; the bytes on the tape are zeros (the CPC original's state) except where said. One player's record (score, water, keys, hammers, level, lives) is a copy at $AAFB of Player1Record $AB09 or Player2Record $AB10.
$A9FD b TakeoffDrawn ; 1 while the take-off picture of the cell the ball leaves is on the screen
$A9FE b SpriteWidth ; The width in bytes of the next picture a blitter draws (the rows go in B)
$A9FF b BallStep ; The step of the ball's current move: 0-15 (bounce, left, right), 0-7 (up, down)
$AA00 b BallState ; The ball's state: 0 bounce in place, 1 left, 2 right, 3 up, 4 down, 5 stopped, 6 burst, 7 fall out, 8 level end
$AA01 w BallSprite ; The ball's current picture (in BallPictures $67E8)
$AA03 w BallScrAddr ; The ball's CPC screen address
$AA05 w BallOldAddr ; The address the ball was drawn at last, to erase it
$AA07 w CellIndex ; A cell's index as a word: the build's and the brick test's current cell
$AA09 s Unused1 ; two bytes never used
$AA0B b CellByte ; Scratch: a cell's byte during the build, a brick's layers in the brick test
$AA0C b FireTick ; Counts frames to the flames' next frame (every fourth)
$AA0D b WallTick ; Counts frames to wall 4's next frame (every fourth)
$AA0E b ExitTick ; Counts frames to the exit's next frame
$AA0F b ExitFrame ; The exit's frame, 1-4
$AA10 w ExitAnimAddr ; The exit's screen address
$AA12 b ExitOpen ; 1 once the exit is open
$AA13 b SpringArmed ; 1 when the up key or fire armed the spring: the next landing goes up (state 3)
$AA14 b CrumblePending ; A crumbling platform waits for the ball to leave
$AA15 b CrumbleActive ; A crumbling platform's animation runs
$AA16 b FireFrame ; The flames' frame, 1-3
$AA17 b WallFrame ; Wall 4's frame, 1-4
$AA18 w LockAnimAddr ; The screen address of the lock being opened
$AA1A w LockAnimSteps ; The lock animation's step table ($C592, $C596 or $C59A)
$AA1C b LockStep ; The lock animation's step
$AA1D b LockActive ; A lock animation runs
$AA1E s Unused2 ; two bytes never used
$AA20 b LevelRecord 12 ; The current level's record, copied from LevelRecords: 36 cells, then BallCell, PrizesLeft, ExitCell
> BuildLevel $CF2E clears $AA20-$AAFA (219 bytes) and copies the 75-byte record here, so the record's byte 36 becomes BallCell $AA44, byte 37 PrizesLeft $AA45, byte 38 ExitCell $AA46 and bytes 39-74 CellObjects $AA47.
$AA44 b BallCell ; The ball's cell, 0-35 (the record's start cell at a life's start)
$AA45 b PrizesLeft ; The prizes still to take; at 0 OpenExit $BCB9 opens the exit
$AA46 b ExitCell ; The cell the exit opens in
$AA47 b CellObjects 12 ; The object of each cell, 0 none, 1-40 (taken ones become 0)
$AA6B b CellPlatforms 12 ; The platform type of each cell (the record's low nibbles; changed as platforms crumble, burn out or break)
$AA8F b CellWalls 12 ; The wall type to the right of each cell (the record's high nibbles; lowered as keys open locks)
$AAB3 b CellBurning 12 ; 1 for a cell whose platform burns (types 8 and 9)
$AAD7 b BrickLayers 12 ; The brick layers still standing in each cell (8 for a whole brick, type 10)
$AAFB w Score ; The current player's score
$AAFD b Water ; Water drops: one puts out a fire the ball lands on
$AAFE b Keys ; Keys: one opens a lock one step
$AAFF b Hammers ; Hammers: one breaks a whole brick's first layer
$AB00 b Level ; The level, 0-99 (shown as is)
$AB01 b Lives ; Spare lives
$AB02 b AfterLives ; Read with the record copies, never written: the byte after the player's record
$AB03 s Unused3 ; never used
$AB04 w Timer ; The level's timer: 1000 at a life's start, one off a frame; the rest goes into the score at the level end
$AB06 b WaterAtStart ; Water at the level's start (given back when a life is lost)
$AB07 b KeysAtStart ; Keys at the level's start
$AB08 b HammersAtStart ; Hammers at the level's start
$AB09 b Player1Record 7 ; Player 1's record: score (word), water, keys, hammers, level, lives ($FA lives: the player is out)
$AB10 b Player2Record 7 ; Player 2's record, the same layout
$AB17 b BorderFlash ; Set by the bottle (an extra life): the CPC border flash, a RET on the Spectrum
$AB18 b HudColumn ; The column the next HUD figure prints at
$AB19 s Unused4 ; never used
$AB1A b StartCell ; The level's start cell (the record's byte 36)
$AB1B b CrumbleCell ; The cell of the crumbling platform
$AB1C b CellPlatform ; The platform type of the ball's cell (CellPlatforms[BallCell], set by $BB32)
$AB1D b CellObject ; The object in the ball's cell
$AB1E b CellWall ; The wall of the ball's cell (written by the unrun ReadWallCell $BB4A)
$AB1F s Unused5 ; never used
$AB20 b CrumbleNewType ; The type the crumbling platform becomes
$AB21 b CrumbleStep ; The crumble animation's step
$AB22 w CrumbleSteps ; The crumble animation's step table
$AB24 w CrumbleAddr ; The crumbling platform's screen address
$AB26 b FireOutStep ; The fire put-out animation's step
$AB27 w FireOutSteps ; Its step table ($C55E, $C56E or $C57E)
$AB29 w FireOutAddr ; Its screen address
$AB2B b FireOutActive ; A fire put-out animation runs
$AB2C s Unused6 ; never used
$AB2D w CellScrAddr ; The CPC screen address of the current cell (CellAddresses $E131)
$AB2F w WallScrAddr ; The CPC screen address of the current wall (WallAddresses $E179)
$AB31 w BrickLayerAddr ; The screen address of the brick layer being knocked off
$AB33 b BrickActive ; A brick animation runs
$AB34 b BrickAnimStep ; The brick animation's step
$AB35 b SpringRecord 8 ; The spring record of the ball's platform (24 bytes from SpringPics $C5D6): 4 offsets, 4 pictures, 4 widths, 4 row counts
$AB4D b TakeoffRecord 6 ; The take-off record (18 bytes from TakeoffPics $C64E): 3 offsets, 3 pictures, 3 widths, 3 row counts
$AB5F s Unused7 ; 29 zero bytes never used
@module setup ; Start-up and the start of a game and of each life: players, records, the cheat, the "ready?" screen
@about GameStart ($AB7C, the BASIC's RANDOMIZE USR 43900) disables interrupts and enters the menu loop at GameRestart: the menu (Menu $EF33) returns the choice in $B3B1 (0 one player, 1 the demo, 2 two players). A game clears the screen, draws the side panels, zeroes both player records ($AB09, $AB10: score word, water, keys, hammers, level, lives), offers the cheat (R, SPACE and V held at once) and starts the first life at LifeStart.
@about LifeStart runs at the start of every level and after every life lost: it resets the ball, sets the timer to 1000, copies the current player's record to the working copy at $AAFB, builds the level (CF2E), prints "ready?" and waits for a key, whose direction sets the first move. The main loop follows.

$AB7C c GameStart ; The code block's entry (USR 43900): DI, then GameRestart
> The BASIC's RANDOMIZE USR 43900 lands here; it disables the interrupts for good (the game waits for frames with EI: HALT, WaitFrame $B6E4) and falls into GameRestart. Nothing jumps back here.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$AB7D c GameRestart ; The menu, then a new game or the demo by the menu's choice $B3B1
> Calls Menu $EF33, then clears the demo flag $ABA6 and the cheat-mode flag $ADDD. By $B3B1: 0 starts a one-player game ($ADDC = 1), 2 a two-player game ($ADDC = 2), 1 the demo ($ABA6 = 1, the demo input pointer $ABA4 = $D25A, one byte before the demo's moves at $D25B; the demo plays one player). Quitting a game (R in MainLoop, QuitToMenu $B7B2) and the end of the demo come back here.
> Witness: L1 7 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname).
$AB80 c GameRestartChoice ; Back from the menu: start what $B3B1 chose
> GameRestart's tail, where Menu returns. It is an entry of its own because the recordings that start inside the menu (work/stage1/probe/load-fast-nokemp/end.szx: menu, gameover, twoname) reach it by Menu's RET with no CALL seen. Clears DemoFlag $ABA6 and CheatMode $ADDD and branches on MenuChoice $B3B1 as GameRestart says.
> Witness: runs after every Menu call (GameRestart L1 7: c1, gameover, menu, twoname).
$ABA3 b UnusedByteABA3 ; One byte ($01) between the JP and the demo variables; nothing reads it (leftover)
$ABA4 w DemoInputPtr ; The demo's input pointer: the last move state read from $D25B+ (BallStopped $B11A)
$ABA6 b DemoFlag ; 1 while the demo plays, 0 in a game: no keys are read, the "ready?" text is skipped
$ABA7 b ObjectFlag ; A flag the object handlers ($B9A7-$B9FD) read; no recording writes it (always 0 in the image)
$ABA8 c NewGameOnePlayer ; One player ($ADDC = 1); NewGame follows
> Witness: L1 7 (c1, gameover, menu).
$ABAF NewGameTwoPlayers ; two players ($ADDC = 2)
$ABB4 NewGame ; white ink on black, the side panels, zeroed records, the cheat test
> NewGame: ClearAttrs $B471 (ink 7, black border), DrawSides $B488, InitPlayers $BB96 (both records zeroed, 2 lives each by $BBA8). Then the cheat: TestKey $B6CB for R ($2B), SPACE ($00) and V ($3C); all three held calls $BB56 (99 water, keys, hammers and lives for player 1). The current player $ADDB = 1. Witness of the cheat test: $ABCB (V tested once R and SPACE are held) ran once, in the menu build.
$ABD3 FirstPlayer ; the current player $ADDB = 1
$ABD8 c LifeStart ; The start of a level or of a new life: resets the ball, the timer and the player's working copy, builds the level, "ready?"
> Entered from NewGame, from the level end (LevelEnd $B2C2 through $B3AE) and after a life lost (LoseLife $BA93). If the cheat mode $ADDD is on (never: its switch is gone, see CheatModeOn $ADE0) $BB7D refills the stores. ClearPlayArea $B4CE, then zeroes the ball state $AA00, BallStep $A9FF, SpringArmed $AA13, the animation flags $AA12, $AB33, $AB2B, $AA15, $AA14, $AA1D, the platform under the ball $AB1C and $A9FD; the timer $AB04 = 1000 ($03E8: the frames the level allows).
> The players: the current player $ADDB is kept in $ADDE. With two players ($ADDC = 2) the code copies player 2's record ($AB10) to the working copy $AAFB and sets $ADDB = 2, then player 1's ($AB09) and $ADDB = 1, each time testing lives = $FA (the mark of a player out) with a JR Z to the very next line: the test does nothing (known only from the code). Then $ADDB = $ADDE again and the record of the current player is copied to $AAFB (7 bytes: score word, water, keys, hammers, level, lives), with the text to show: ReadyText $AD3B (one player), Player1ReadyText $AD48 or Player2ReadyText $AD84.
> In a game: ClearPlayArea, BuildLevel $CF2E, PrintText $E667 of that text; in the demo BuildLevel only. Then water, keys and hammers are kept in $AB06-$AB08 (the stores at the level's start), $BB32 reads the platform under the ball, and D ($32) calls CheatModeSwitch $ADDF (a RET). A game then draws the HUD ($E58B), waits for all keys up (WaitRelease $B401) and for a key (WaitPressHeld $B6DD), and the key sets the first move (DecideMove $B7E4). Last, in both: ClearPlayArea and BuildLevel again (the text goes), the HUD and WaitFrame $B6E4; MainLoop follows.
> Witness: L1 57 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$AC19 ; two players: player 2's record to the working copy, $ADDB = 2
$AC30 LifeStartPlayer1 ; player 1's record to the working copy, $ADDB = 1
$AC47 LifeStartCurrent ; the current player back from $ADDE; pick the record and the text
$AC63 LifeStartPlayer2 ; player 2: record $AB10, text Player2ReadyText (no recording ran it)
$AC69 LifeStartCopy ; the record to the working copy $AAFB
$AC86 LifeStartDemo ; the demo: BuildLevel without the text
$AC89 LifeStartStores ; keep water, keys and hammers in $AB06-$AB08
$ACB0 LifeStartReady ; the HUD, wait for no key, then for a key; DecideMove
$ACBC LifeStartGo ; the play area again without the text, the HUD, one frame

@module mainloop ; The main loop, the state dispatcher and the keys
@about MainLoop $ACC8 runs once a frame: it reads the ball's cell, runs the animations (flames, walls, conveyors, bricks, locks), counts the timer down, the bottle's pause, tests R (quit), D (the gone cheat mode), the pause key (slot $F08A), waits for the frame (WaitFrame $B6E4 through $B87E) and jumps to the ball's state routine through StateTable $AD29 by BallState $AA00. Every state routine ends with JP MainLoop.
@about MainLoop ran 81737 times over the recordings (its witness), one pass a frame, so the game moves at 50 steps a second (the ball's bounce in place, 16 steps, takes 16 frames).

$ACC8 c MainLoop ; One frame of the game: the animations, the timer, the keys, the frame wait, then the ball's state routine
> Each pass: $BB32 (the platform under the ball to $AB1C), $BB05 (the ball's cell address), the animations $BE94 ($AB33), $BFF3 ($AB2B), $C19D ($AA15), $C43E ($AA1D), $BBCD and $BC30 (every fourth frame: the flames and the walls), $BD21 ($AA12), the timer $B7D1 (the timer $AB04 minus one), the bottle's pause $BA60. Then the keys: R ($2B) held jumps to QuitToMenu $B7B2; D ($32) calls CheatModeSwitch $ADDF (a RET); in a game (not the demo) the pause key (slot $F08A) calls Pause $B440. Last WaitFrame (through $B87E) and the dispatch.
> Witness: L1 92281 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$AD03 MainLoopPause ; in a game: the pause key
$AD0C MainLoopWait ; the frame wait
$AD0F Dispatch ; jump to StateTable[BallState AND 15]
$AD29 w StateTable 9 ; The ball's nine state routines by BallState $AA00: 0 bounce, 1 left, 2 right, 3 up, 4 down, 5 stopped, 6 burst, 7 out below, 8 level end
$AD3B b ReadyText 16 ; The one-player text: AT 11,13, INK 7, double height, "ready?", end (PrintText $E667)
$AD48 b Player1ReadyText 16 ; The two-player text for player 1: "PLAYER 1" at 9,12 in ink 5, "ready?" at 12,13 in ink 7, "PLAYER" down column 31 in ink 5, then "1" at 17,31 in ink 7
$AD84 b Player2ReadyText 16 ; The same for player 2: "PLAYER 2", "ready?", "PLAYER" down column 31, "2"
$ADBF b BlankReadyText 16 ; Spaces over "PLAYER n" and "ready?" (AT 9,12 and 12,13); nothing prints it (leftover)
$ADDB b CurrentPlayer ; The player playing now: 1 or 2 (selects the ball sprite, PlayerBallSprite $ADE6, and the record)
$ADDC b PlayerCount ; The number of players, 1 or 2 (GameRestart)
$ADDD b CheatMode ; The cheat mode: non-zero refills the stores each life ($BB7D) and lets the level end pick a level ($B2C9); no code sets it (CheatModeOn $ADE0 is cut off)
$ADDE b SavedPlayer ; LifeStart's copy of CurrentPlayer while it walks the two records
$ADDF c CheatModeSwitch ; The D key's routine: a RET, put before the code that would switch the cheat mode on
> MainLoop and LifeStart call it when D ($32) is held. The RET makes the key do nothing; the next six bytes (CheatModeOn $ADE0) are the original body. Witness: called whenever D is held; no recording held D.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$ADE0 c CheatModeOn ; LD A,1: LD ($ADDD),A: RET: the cut-off body of the D key (never runs)
> Would set CheatMode $ADDD = 1. CheatModeSwitch's RET in front of it keeps it from running; nothing else jumps here (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().

@module ball ; The ball: the sprite routines, the move states, landing, the burst, falling out and the level end
@about The ball is a CPC-style screen address $AA03 (the previous one in $AA05) and a sprite $AA01 (the round ball $67E8 or a rolling frame; player 2's sprites lie $280 higher). Each move state takes one step a frame from a table of address offsets: bounce in place (BallBounce, 16 steps, 18 lines up and back), left and right (16 steps: the same arc and 10 CPC bytes, 5 Spectrum bytes, sideways), up and down (8 steps of 4 lines: 32 lines, one cell). Before each step a per-step check from the state's handler table ($AE9B, $AF15, $AF8F, $B044, $B0FA: routines at $C778-$CECC) tests the cells the ball crosses (walls, platforms, objects) and continues in the state's move routine.
@about At the end of a move LandingCheck $B09E decides: on a platform, DecideMove $B7E4 picks the next state from the platform and the keys; in the air the ball falls (state 4); with no platform on the bottom row it falls out of the screen (state 7). The S key ($31) kills the ball in any move state (Suicide $BA71). Death by the burst (state 6) or by falling out (state 7) ends in LoseLife $BA93.

$ADE6 c PlayerBallSprite ; HL = the sprite at HL for the current player: + $280 for player 2
> Input HL a ball sprite (player 1's); output HL unchanged when CurrentPlayer $ADDB = 1, else HL + $280 (player 2's ball, a different pattern). Player 2's branch ran in no recording (no two-player game reached play).
> Witness: L1 218172 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$ADF1 c DrawBallRound ; XOR the round ball ($67E8) at the ball's address $AA03
> Witness: L1 57057 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$ADFE c DrawBallFrame ; XOR the ball's sprite $AA01 (a rolling frame) at $AA03
> Witness: L1 34344 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$AE0B c DrawBallRoundOld ; XOR the round ball at the previous address $AA05 (erases it)
> Witness: L1 200 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname, l10, l51, l68, l96, rzx x29).
$AE18 EraseBallOld ; XOR the sprite $AA01 at the previous address $AA05
