@module menu ; The menu's choice and the controls: the keyboard and the Kempston stick
$B3B1 b MenuChoice ; The menu's choice: 0 one player, 1 the demo, 2 two players (written by Menu at $EF97, $F0C7)
$B3B2 c ReadControlMask ; Builds a byte of the eight key slots held (bit 7 the last) into $B3CE: never called
$B3B5 ReadControlMaskKeepDE
$B3BA ReadControlMaskLoop
$B3C6 ReadControlMaskNext
> For the slots KeySlots $F084-$F08B in turn: E shifts right and bit 7 is set when TestKeyBit says the slot's key is held; the word DE goes to $B3CE. Nothing calls it and nothing reads $B3CE (known only from the code): a left-over of a control reader the game replaced by one TestKey call a slot.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$B3CE w ControlMask ; ReadControlMask's result; never read (leftover)
$B3D0 c TestKeyBit ; Z when the key of code A is held: patches BIT b,(IX+d) at $B3F3 with d = A >> 3 and b = A AND 7
> Input A a key code: row (A >> 3) 0-8 of KeyRows $B436 and bit (A AND 7). Rows 0-7 are the half-rows $7FFE, $BFFE, $DFFE, $EFFE, $F7FE, $FBFE, $FDFE, $FEFE (so $00 SPACE, $2B R, $31 S, $32 D, $3C V); row 8 is the Kempston stick ($40 right, $41 left, $42 down, $43 up, $44 fire). Writes the displacement to $B3F5 and the bit into the opcode byte $B3F6 (self-modifying), then tests. Output Z when held (bit 0), NZ when not. Keeps IX.
> Witness: L1 423995 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B3F0 c TestKeyBitTest ; TestKeyBit's second half: stores the patched opcode (A) at $B3F6 and runs BIT b,(IX+d)
> The walk makes it an entry of its own (the recordings count one run more here than at TestKeyBit: a recording that starts inside the routine). Nothing calls or jumps here (known only from the code); it is the tail of TestKeyBit.
> Witness: L1 423996 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B3FA c WaitPress ; Waits until a key or the stick is held, then until all are released
> Witness: L1 14595 (menu).
$B401 c WaitRelease ; Waits until no key and no stick direction is held
> Witness: L1 152206 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$B407 c AnyControl ; ReadControls, then Z when nothing is held (the AND of the nine KeyRows bytes is $FF)
> Witness: L1 501803 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x26).
$B411 AnyControlLoop ; AND the nine rows
$B418 c ReadControls ; Reads the 8 keyboard half-rows into KeyRows $B436-$B43D and the Kempston port $DF into $B43E
> DI, then IN ($FE) for each half-row, $7FFE first, each stored with bits 5-7 set (OR $E0). Then IN A,($DF) (A, the high byte, holds the last row's value): the low five bits are the Kempston stick, 1 = pressed. When all five are 1 (no interface: the port floats at $FF) the routine returns with interrupts still off and $B43E unchanged; else it stores the complement (0 = pressed, like the keys) and EI. A Kempston mouse answering on port $DF would be read as a stick here (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 925798 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B420 ReadControlsLoop ; one half-row
$B436 b KeyRows 9 ; The last read of the controls: 8 keyboard half-rows ($7FFE ... $FEFE) and the Kempston stick, 0 = held
$B43F b UnusedByteB43F ; One byte after KeyRows; nothing reads it (leftover)
$B440 c Pause ; The pause key: wait for its release, then for a key and its release
> Called by MainLoop when the pause key (slot $F08A) is held in a game. Witness: 3 pauses in the recordings.
> Witness: L1 3 (menu).
$B447 c TestLeft ; NZ when the left control (slot $F086) is held
> Witness: L1 5463 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx77, rzx79-rzx99).
$B44E c TestRight ; NZ when the right control (slot $F087) is held
> Witness: L1 4674 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx77, rzx79-rzx99).
$B455 c TestFire ; NZ when the fire control (slot $F084) is held: never called
> TestUpOrFire $B463 tests fire itself; nothing calls this one (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$B45C c TestDown ; NZ when the down control (slot $F085) is held
> Witness: L1 990 (l x12, rzx x94).
$B463 c TestUpOrFire ; NZ when up (slot $F088) or fire (slot $F084) is held: the spring
> Witness: L1 6352 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).

@module render ; The screen: attributes, the side panels, the play area and the CPC-to-Spectrum line table
@about The game draws with Amstrad CPC screen addresses ($C000 + line AND 7 x $800 + row x 80 + byte). LineAddress $B515 turns the line and column ScreenAddrToLine $C71F found into a Spectrum address: SpectrumLines $B52D gives the Spectrum line of each of the 200 CPC lines (the first 7 and the last 8 go to a scratch area at $FCE0, off the Spectrum screen), $B52C the Spectrum byte column (the CPC byte column / 2).

$B471 c ClearAttrs ; All attributes = A (7: white ink, black paper), the border = A >> 3 (black)
> Witness: L1 9 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname).
$B488 c DrawSides ; Draws the side panels: $6398 at the CPC address $F800 and $6518 at $F83C, 192 lines each ($E318)
> Witness: L1 9 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname).
$B4A8 c FillColumn ; Stores C into B lines of one byte column from the CPC address DE: never called
$B4B5 FillColumnLoop
> Converts DE ($C71F, $B515) and stores C at each line's Spectrum address. No code calls it (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$B4CE c ClearPlayArea ; Clears columns 1-29 of the 200 lines (the blank $6708), then the play attributes (ink 7)
> Witness: L1 151 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$B4D6 ClearPlayAreaLoop ; one line
$B4F8 c SetPlayAttrs ; Each attribute row: column 0 and 30 yellow (6), columns 1-29 = A; column 31 untouched
> Witness: L1 149 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$B4FD SetPlayAttrsLoop ; one attribute row
$B515 c LineAddress ; IX = SpectrumLines + 2 x the line ($C751), $B52C = the CPC column ($C750) / 2
> Input the line $C751 and column $C750 from ScreenAddrToLine $C71F. Output IX points at the Spectrum line address; the caller ORs $B52C into its low byte.
> Witness: L1 324616 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B52C b SpectrumColumn ; LineAddress's Spectrum byte column (the CPC byte column / 2)
$B52D w SpectrumLines 8 ; The Spectrum address of the start of each CPC line 0-206: 7 lines at $FCE0, the 192 screen lines from $4000, 8 more at $FCE0

