@module mainloop ; The main loop, the frame wait and the state dispatcher
@about The frame wait and the small timing helpers the game, the death and the game-over paths share.
$B6E4 c WaitFrame ; Refresh the HUD's attributes and wait for the next frame interrupt
> Calls $E58B (the per-frame HUD refresh, which repaints the attributes with PaintHudAttrs $B6EE), then EI and HALT: the game runs at one pass of MainLoop $ACC8 a frame. IX is preserved. Called by MainLoop ($ACC5) and WaitOneFrame $B87E.
> Witness: L1 95897 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).

@module hud ; The timer, lives, score, HUD, game over and hall of fame
@about The HUD's attribute repaint, the level timer and the loss of a life.
$B6EE c PaintHudAttrs ; Repaint the attributes of the HUD row and of column 30
> Copies the 20 bytes of HudRowAttrs $B730 to the top attribute row from $5803, in groups of 5, 3, 3, 3, 3 and 3 with one or two cells skipped between them (the cells between the HUD's fields keep their colour), then writes 6 (yellow on black) down column 30 ($581E, 24 rows). Called by $E58B every frame, so whatever the CPC-style drawing leaves in those cells is put right once a frame.
> Witness: L1 95989 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B723 ; HL = the attribute cell after the last group: row 0, column 30
$B72B PaintColumn30 ; 24 rows, 32 bytes apart
$B730 b HudRowAttrs 16 ; The attributes of the HUD row (top line), read by PaintHudAttrs
> 20 attributes: 5 of $07 (white on black) for $5803-$5807, then five groups of three, each one coloured icon cell ($46 bright yellow, $45 bright cyan, $45, $46, $42 bright red) and two white digits. Each group is the icon and number of one HUD field (known only from the code which field is which: $E5A1-$E5F9 print them).
$B744 HudRowAttrsEnd ; three unread $C9 bytes before FirmwareStub
> Three $C9 (RET) bytes no code reads or runs: part of the row of RET stubs that ends at $B754 (see FirmwareStub $B747).

@module mainloop
$B747 c FirmwareStub ; A RET in place of an Amstrad CPC firmware call
> A lone RET. $B744-$B754 is a row of 17 $C9 bytes: the Spectrum port replaced the CPC firmware jump block entries the game called with RETs at the same kind of place. This one is called with B = C = 26 by TakeBottle $BA1C and with B = C = 0 by BottleFlashOff $BA60: on the CPC, B and C are two colours, which fits SCR SET BORDER (26 bright white, 0 black): a border flash for the extra life that the Spectrum drops (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 40 (menu, l10, l36, l45, l68, rzx x15).
$B748 b FirmwareStubs2 ; Eight more RET stubs ($C9), none called
> Never run or read.
$B750 c FirmwareStub3 ; A RET stub, called by $B387 at the end of a level
> A lone RET in the row of firmware stubs ($B744-$B754); called by the level-end code at $B387.
> Witness: L1 20 (menu, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x15).
$B751 b FirmwareStubs4 ; Three more RET stubs ($C9), none called
> Never run or read.
$B754 c FirmwareStub5 ; A RET stub, called only by the disabled cheat routes $B2C9 and $BA80
> A lone RET; both callers are dead code (the level-select cheat, see SelfDestruct $BA71), so it never runs.
> Witness: L1 0 ().

@module hiscore ; Game over, the hall of fame and the return to the menu
@about The end of a player's game: the hall of fame check, the two-player hand-over and the way back to the menu.
$B755 c GameOver ; A player has lost his last ball: hall of fame, mark him out, hand over or end the game
> Entered from LoseLife $BA93 when the lives ($AB01) are 0. IX = the record of the player in play: $AB09 for player 1 ($ADDB = 1), $AB10 for player 2. When the score ($AAFB) is not below the lowest hall of fame score (the word at $EF25), NameEntry $EC40 runs. Then the record's lives byte (IX+6) is set to $FA, the mark of a player out of the game.
> One player ($ADDC = 1), or the other player already out ($FA in his record): GameOverPause $B7A9 and back to the menu. Otherwise the game passes to the other player through LoseLife's hand-over ($BADF or $BAF1). Witness: the game-over recording (stage1/gameover) ends here; the RZX never loses its last life; the two-player branches ran in stage2/rec/twoname (both players to the end: player 1 out, the hand-over $BADF/$BAF1 to player 2, then the menu).
> Witness: L1 4 (gameover, twoname).
$B764 GameOverHiScore ; compare the score with the hall of fame's last entry
$B77A GameOverMarkOut ; mark the player out ($FA)
$B796 GameOverPlayer1 ; player 1 was in play: is player 2 still in?
$B7A9 c GameOverPause ; Wait about 2.5 seconds, then back to the menu
> Twenty times GameOverDelay $C4D5 (each about 40 000 delay loops and a frame), then BackToMenu $B7B2. Entered from GameOver $B755 and from the end of the demo ($B3A8).
> Witness: L1 3 (gameover, menu, twoname).
$B7AB GameOverPauseLoop
$B7B2 c BackToMenu ; Wait up to a second for a key, then restart at the menu
> WaitKeyOrSecond $B7B8, then JP $AB7D (the menu and a new game). Entered from GameOverPause and from MainLoop when the quit key ($2B, see $ACEC) is down.
> Witness: L1 6 (gameover, menu, twoname).

@module mainloop
$B7B8 c WaitKeyOrSecond ; Wait 50 frames or until a key is pressed
> Sets the counter $EFED to 50, then a frame at a time (EI, HALT) calls AnyKey $B407 and returns as soon as it reports a key (NZ), else counts down to 0. Used between lives (LoseLife $BA93), after a level ($B3AB) and before the menu (BackToMenu).
> Witness: L1 56 (fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x26).
$B7BE WaitKeyOrSecondLoop
$B7D0 WaitKeyOrSecondEnd

@module hud
$B7D1 c TickTimer ; Count the level timer down by one frame, stopping at 0
> The level timer $AB04 (a word, 1000 = $03E8 at each level start, $AC06) goes down by one each frame MainLoop runs ($ACE4) and stops at 0. Nothing here acts on 0: the timer only sets the bonus the level end ($B35F) adds to the score. 1000 frames are 20 seconds.
> Witness: L1 92279 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B7D9 TickTimerDec
$B7DE TickTimerLowByte ; high byte 0: stop at 0

@module ball ; The ball: bounce, moves, platforms and walls
@about What the ball does next at each landing, its rolling frames and its hit on a brick from below.
$B7E4 c ChooseNextState ; At a landing, choose the ball's next state from the platform and the keys
> Called by the bounce handlers ($ACB9, $B009, $B0AE, $B0CB, $B0DA) when the ball lands on a cell. In play ($ABA6 = 0), in this order: SpringArmed $AA13 = 1 gives state 3 (up); the cell's platform ($AB1C) $0B, the open exit, gives 8 (level end); 7 gives 2 (right: conveyor); 6 gives 1 (left: conveyor); 5 gives 5 (stopped, waiting for left or right); $0A (bricks) keeps the state; the left key (KeySlots $F086, LeftKey $B447) gives 1; the right key ($F087, RightKey $B44E) gives 2; a ball going up (3) with the down key ($F085, DownKey $B45C) gives 0, and without it stays 3; anything else gives 0 (bounce in place).
> In the demo ($ABA6 not 0) the exit and the conveyors are checked first, then the next byte of the demo moves (the pointer $ABA4 is advanced) becomes BallState $AA00, and SpringArmed still forces 3. Output: BallState $AA00. Witness: every play recording (rzx x100, menu demo).
> Witness: L1 6161 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B7FF ChooseDemoState ; demo: the next byte of the demo moves
$B812 ChoosePlayState
$B843 SetStateBounce ; state 0
$B849 ChooseUpOrBounce ; going up: the down key turns it into a bounce
$B84F c SetStateLeft ; BallState = 1 (moving left)
> Also the target of the left conveyor and of the stopped ball's left key ($B13F).
> Witness: L1 979 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x11, rzx00-rzx17, rzx21-rzx81, rzx83-rzx86, rzx88-rzx99).
$B855 c SetStateRight ; BallState = 2 (moving right)
> Also the target of the right conveyor and of the stopped ball's right key ($B139).
> Witness: L1 1238 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$B85B SetStateUp ; state 3
$B861 c SetStateDown ; BallState = 4 (moving down): never called
> LD A,4: LD ($AA00),A: RET, like its neighbours, but no code jumps here (known only from the code: a leftover of ChooseNextState).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$B867 c SetStateStopped ; BallState = 5 (stopped on a type 5 platform)
> Witness: L1 141 (l x6, rzx x26).
$B86D SetStateLevelEnd ; state 8

