@module hiscore
$C4D5 c GameOverDelay ; Two Delay(200) and a frame: about 2 × 130 000 T-states
> Called 20 times by GameOverPause $B7A9.
> Witness: L1 60 (gameover, menu, twoname).
@module ball ; The ball: the bounce, the moves left, right, up and down, the platforms and walls it meets
@about The ball's tables and the step handlers of its five moving states. Each state (BallState $AA00, 0 bounce in place, 1 left, 2 right, 3 up, 4 down) has a table of 16 (8 for up and down) step handlers in the main loop's range ($AE9B, $AF15, $AF8F, $B044, $B0FA); the handler for BallStep $A9FF runs once a frame, then the state's common tail moves and draws the ball. Step 0 of each state looks at the platform the ball leaves (the cell's platform type $AB1C, which $BB32 reads from $AA6B[ball cell $AA44]) and dispatches through a table of 13 handlers, one a platform type.
@about The cells are a grid of 6 columns and 6 rows (cell = row x 6 + column, 0-35): the cell below is +6, the one above -6. The wall between a cell and the next one to the right has the cell's index in the wall array $AA8F.
$C4E2 w BallFramesRight 16 ; The ball's picture for each of the 16 steps of the move right
> MoveRight's draw ($B882) sets the ball sprite $AA01 = BallFramesRight[BallStep]: the 15 rolling frames $6810-$6A40 (40 bytes apart), then the round ball $67E8 at step 15. Read 16 359 times in the recordings.
$C502 w BallFramesLeft 16 ; The ball's picture for each of the 16 steps of the move left
> The same frames in the reverse order ($6A40 down to $6810, then $67E8), read by $B8A2 for the move left (13 276 times).
$C522 w FireFrames 4 ; The four pictures of the flames on a burning platform
> $BD21 draws FireFrames[$AA0F - 1] ($80D5, $80F3, $8111, $812F; 30 bytes apart, 5 lines of 6 bytes) with ink $5E at the burning cell, every second frame (5672 times).
$C52A w SpringCurves 5 ; The spring-press records of platform types 1-5 (24 bytes each at SpringPics)
> LoadSpring $BD73 copies the record of the ball's platform type (1-5) to $AB35 when the up key or fire arms the spring. The records are at SpringPics $C5D6.

@module render ; The renderer: the platforms, walls, objects and their animations on the CPC-layout screen
@about The game draws in Amstrad CPC screen addresses ($C000 + line in the character row x $800 + character row x 80 + byte column) and converts each one to a Spectrum line with ScreenAddrToLine $C71F and the line table $B52D. The pictures are CPC mode-1 bytes; the Spectrum shows their low nibbles (XOR $E3EE, store $E318, with ink $E3E5).
@about The animations below are lists of routines, one a step: a player calls entry N at its N-th call and stops when an entry clears its active flag.
$C534 w BrickBreakSteps 4 ; The four steps of a brick wall breaking
> $BE94 calls BrickBreakSteps[$AB34] once a frame while $AB33 is set: $BEBB, $BF19, $BF60, $BFA7 (1476 calls).
$C53C w CrumbleSteps2 4 ; The steps of a type-2 platform turning into type 3 after the ball leaves it
> Set by $C3D5 ($AB22); $C1C3 writes the new type ($B8C2: $AA6B[$AB1B] = $AB20 = 3), the others redraw. Witness: the RZX, 220 starts.
$C544 w CrumbleSteps3 4 ; The steps of a type-3 platform turning into type 4
> Set by $C3F8 (328 starts); the new type $AB20 = 4.
$C54C w CrumbleSteps4 9 ; The steps of a type-4 platform vanishing
> Set by $C41B (672 starts); the new type $AB20 = 0, no platform; the last step $C24C clears $AA14 and $AA15.
$C55E w FireOutLeft 8 ; The steps of the water putting out a fire, the ball moving left
> LeftStep9 $C9CE starts it ($AB27, $AB26 = 0, $AB29 the cell's screen address, $AB2B = 1) when the ball reaches a burning platform (type 8 or 9) and holds a water drop.
$C56E w FireOutRight 8 ; The same, the ball moving right
> Started by RightStep9 $CC11.
$C57E w FireOutDown 9 ; The same, the ball falling onto the burning platform below
> Started by DownStep0 $CE24.
$C590 w LeftoverWord ; $B1CB, a word nothing reads
> Known only from the recordings: no read. It would be a tenth step of FireOutDown ($B1CB is inside the burst animation $B1A7-$B1E6).
$C592 w LockOpenSteps3 2 ; The two steps of a lock of type 3 opening to type 2
> The ball hits the lock with a key: $AA1A points here, $C43E plays it ($AA1C the step, $AA1D active). $C464: sound $F43E, XOR out the type-3 picture, XOR the flash picture; $C46E: XOR the flash picture off, draw the type-2 picture, stop.
$C596 w LockOpenSteps2 2 ; The two steps of a lock of type 2 opening to type 1
> $C476 (sound $F455, XOR out the type-2 picture, flash), $C480 (flash off, draw the type-1 picture, stop).
$C59A w LockOpenSteps1 7 ; The seven steps of a lock of type 1 opening for good
> $C488 (sound $F46C, XOR out the type-1 picture, flash), $C492 (flash off, store $7FDC), then $800C, $803C, $806C, $809C and the blank $6708 stored over the wall's place ($AA18): the lock falls away. 97 openings in the recordings.
$C5A8 w PlatformDrawTable 13 ; The routine that draws each platform type 0-12 when the level is built
> BuildLevel's cell loop ($CFC8) jumps to PlatformDrawTable[type]; each routine draws at the cell's screen address $AB2D and goes on to BuildLevelObject $CFF4. Type 0 and type 11 (the exit) draw nothing: the exit appears only when OpenExit $BCB9 writes it.
$C5C2 w WallDrawTable 5 ; The routine that draws each wall type 0-4 when the level is built
> BuildLevelObject jumps to WallDrawTable[$AA8F[cell]]; each goes on to BuildLevelNextCell $D047.

@module ball
$C5CC w TakeoffTable 5 ; The take-off picture record of platform types 1-5 (18 bytes each at TakeoffPics)
> LeftTakeoff $C890 and RightTakeoff $CAD6 copy TakeoffTable[type - 1]'s record to $AB4D.
$C5D6 b SpringPics 24 ; The spring-press records of platform types 1-5, 24 bytes each
> Each record is four pictures, the frames of the platform pressed by the bouncing ball: four screen offsets from the cell's address (words), four picture addresses (words), four widths in bytes, four heights in lines. Type 1: offsets 0, $F800, $2FB0, $2FB0; pictures $7788, $7848, $7880, $78C8. LoadSpring $BD73 copies one to $AB35; $BDA6-$BDCE draw its frames.
$C5D6 SpringPics1 ; type 1, the plain platform
$C5EE SpringPics2 ; type 2, crumbling (first stage)
$C606 SpringPics3 ; type 3, crumbling (second stage)
$C61E SpringPics4 ; type 4, crumbling (last stage)
$C636 SpringPics5 ; type 5, the stopping platform
$C64E b TakeoffPics 18 ; The take-off records of platform types 1-5, 18 bytes each
> Each record is three pictures: three screen offsets from the cell's address (words), three picture addresses (words), three widths, three heights. Picture 0 is the platform itself, picture 1 the one drawn when the ball leaves it to the left (the same picture as the conveyor 6, $77A8 for type 1), picture 2 the one for the right ($77F8, as the conveyor 7). $BE43 draws picture 0, $BE5E picture 1, $BE79 picture 2, all by XOR, so step 5 of the move draws them again to undo them.
$C64E TakeoffPics1 ; type 1
$C660 TakeoffPics2 ; type 2
$C672 TakeoffPics3 ; type 3
$C684 TakeoffPics4 ; type 4
