@module levels ; The level builder
@about BuildLevel copies the level's 75-byte record from the level data $8C3C to the work area $AA20, splits the 36 cells into platforms and walls, and draws every cell, object, wall and the ball.
$CF2E c BuildLevel ; Build and draw level Level: copy the record, split the cells, draw them and the ball
> The CPC inks of the level (LevelColours $E119 row Level mod 7 + 1) go to $B6DB, a RET on the Spectrum. The work area $AA20-$AAFA (219 bytes) is cleared, the record at $8C3C + 75 x Level is copied to $AA20 (byte 36, the start cell, also to $AB1A), and each of the 36 cells is split: the high nibble to the walls $AA8F, the low nibble to the platforms $AA6B. Then BuildLevelCell draws every cell. Last the ball: sprite $67E8, cell $AA44 = the start cell, its screen address $AA03 = the cell's + $2F62, drawn by XOR (10 lines of 4 bytes). Called by the main loop at $AC7D, $AC86 and $ACBF (75 builds in the recordings).
> Witness: L1 94 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$CF34 LevelMod7 ; A = Level mod 7 by subtraction
$CF3B LevelColourRow
$CF45 LevelColourSeek
$CF6E ClearWorkArea ; $AA20-$AAFA to 0
$CF81 SeekLevelRecord ; HL = $8BF1 + 75 x (Level + 1) = $8C3C + 75 x Level
$CF98 SplitCell ; cell 36 - B: the high nibble to $AA8F, the low to $AA6B
$CFC8 c BuildLevelCell ; Draw cell 36 - B: its platform through PlatformDrawTable
> $AA44 = the cell, $AA07 = the cell as a word, $AB2D its screen address ($BB05), then a jump to PlatformDrawTable[platform].
> Witness: L1 3363 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$CFF4 c BuildLevelObject ; Draw the cell's object and its wall
