@module menu ; The demo's moves
@about The demo plays these moves instead of the keys.
$D25A b DemoMoves ; The demo's move list: one byte a decision
> GameStart sets the demo pointer $ABA4 = $D25A; each decision reads the byte after it (from $D25B on, 48 bytes read): 0-5 the next ball state at a landing, 9 arm the spring at a step 9 (BounceStep9, LeftStep9 and the others). The first byte is never read.
@res tables/demo-moves.bin
$D28B s DemoPad ; 41 zeros after the demo moves, never read

@module render
$D2B4 b PanelPicture ; A picture of 18 bytes x 200 lines in CPC mode 1, never read
> Only DrawPanelA and DrawPanelB (never called) would read it. 3600 bytes, $D2B4-$E0C3.
@res sprites/panel.bin

@module levels
$E0C4 b LevelLeftover ; 85 bytes of small numbers, never read
> Known only from the recordings: no read and no reader in the code. Pairs of values 0-$16; their meaning is not known.
@res leftovers/e0c4.bin
$E119 b LevelColours 3 ; The CPC inks of the three pens, a row for each level mod 7
> BuildLevel reads row Level mod 7 + 1 (the first row, $E119-$E11B, is never read) and passes the three values (CPC firmware colours 0-26) to $B6DB, which on the Spectrum returns at once: a leftover of the Amstrad version.
@res tables/level-colours.bin

@module render
$E131 b CellAddresses 8 ; The CPC screen address of each cell 0-35 (36 words)
> $BB05 sets $AB2D = the word for the ball's cell $AA44; the fire put-out code reads it too. Rows are $140 bytes ($C942, $CA82, $CBC2, $CD02, $CE42, $CF82 for column 0), columns 10 bytes apart. Words that only look like program addresses.
@res tables/cell-addresses.bin
$E179 b WallAddresses 8 ; The CPC screen address of each wall 0-35 (36 words; the sixth of each row is 0)
> $BB1D sets $AB2F = the word for wall A. Wall N is between cell N and cell N + 1; the last of each row has no wall.
@res tables/wall-addresses.bin

@module ball
$E1C1 b BrickSteps ; The value a brick counter must have at each step 0-7 of a jump for the bricks to break
> $B8D1 compares $AAD7[cell] with BrickSteps[BallStep]: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 8, 7, 6.
@res tables/brick-steps.bin

@module levels
$E1C9 b EditorTexts ; The texts of a level editor's menu, letters coded A = 1, never read
> "RETOUR", "EDITEUR", "CHARGER TABLEAUX", "SAUVER TABLEAUX", "EFFACER TABLEAU", "QUITTER EDITEUR", each ended by 0: the French menu of a level editor the game does not have. Known only from the bytes: nothing reads them.
@res texts/editor.bin
$E21A s EditorFontPad ; Four zero bytes before the editor's letters, never read
$E21E b EditorFont 5 ; The letters A-Z of the editor, 5 lines of 8 pixels each, never read
> 26 one-colour glyphs for EditorTexts. Two bytes ($E277-$E278) are read in the recordings, by no reader of the font: known only from the recordings.
@res sprites/editor-font.bin
@module sprites ; The sprite blitters: CPC mode-1 graphics drawn on the Spectrum screen, and their colour cells
@about The game keeps the Amstrad CPC's graphics and screen addresses. A sprite is CPC mode-1 bytes, ($A9FE) bytes a line (4 pixels each, two bit planes) and B lines; its place is a CPC screen address ($C000 + line%8 x $800 + row x 80 + byte). The blitters turn the address into a pixel line and a CPC byte column ($C71F), take the Spectrum line address from the line table ($B515: IX = $B52D + 2 x line, $B52C = column / 2) and pack two CPC bytes into one Spectrum byte: the low nibbles (one bit plane, pens 2 and 3 show as ink) for the sprites, the high nibbles for the bricks. An odd CPC column starts with a half byte.
@about Store (DrawSprite), XOR (XorSprite) and XOR of the high nibbles (XorBricks), each with a variant that then colours the cells the sprite covers (PaintAttrs). Interrupts are off while a sprite is drawn.
$E2A0 c DrawSpriteColoured ; Draw a sprite (store) and colour the cells it covers
> DrawSprite $E318 (DE the CPC screen address, HL the graphics, B the lines, ($A9FE) the width in CPC bytes), then PaintAttrs $E2A3 with the attribute in C.
> Witness: L1 89434 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx34, rzx36-rzx44, rzx46-rzx99).
$E2A3 c PaintAttrs ; Colour the cells under the sprite just drawn with the attribute C
$E2B5 PaintAttrsSkip
$E2CF PaintAttrsSkip2
$E30C PaintAttrsLoop
$E30E PaintAttrsLoop2
> Input: C the attribute (bit 7 set: the area is 8 lines taller), the place left by $C71F ($C74F the CPC character row, $C750 the CPC byte column), the height in lines at SpriteHeight $E3E4 and the width at $A9FE. Nothing is done when C's ink (bits 0-2) is 0.
> The cells are rows ($C74F - 1) onwards (a sprite starting in CPC row 0, above the Spectrum screen, loses its first 8 lines), columns $C750 / 2 onwards, ((width + column bit 0 + 1) / 2) cells wide and (height + 7) / 8 rows high; each gets C (bit 7 cleared), written into the operand of the LD (HL),n at $E30E before the loop. Output: the attributes; $E3E4 and $C74F changed.
> Witness: L1 275682 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E318 c DrawSprite ; Draw a CPC sprite on the screen, storing its bytes
$E33F DrawSpriteLoop
$E34F DrawSpriteLoop2
$E369 DrawSpriteSkip
$E37C DrawSpriteSkip2
$E38B DrawSpriteLoop3
$E3A8 DrawSpriteLoop4
$E3C2 DrawSpriteSkip3
$E3D5 DrawSpriteSkip4
> Input: DE the CPC screen address, HL the graphics, B the lines, ($A9FE) the width in CPC bytes. B goes to SpriteHeight $E3E4. Each line: the Spectrum address from the line table, then width / 2 bytes, each (first byte << 4) | (second byte & $0F), and for an odd width a last half byte (first byte << 4). An odd CPC column ($C750 bit 0) starts with the low nibble of one byte alone and ends with a high-nibble byte when the width is even. Output: carry clear, BC kept; IY kept; the screen bytes stored. Interrupts are off from $E32A and on again at the end.
> Witness: L1 91481 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E3E4 b SpriteHeight ; The height in lines of the last sprite drawn (PaintAttrs takes it)
$E3E5 c XorSpriteColoured ; XOR a sprite onto the screen and colour its cells
> XorSprite $E3EE (HL the CPC screen address, DE the graphics, B the lines), then PaintAttrs $E2A3 with C.
> Witness: L1 186047 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E3EE c XorSprite ; XOR a CPC sprite onto the screen
$E413 XorSpriteLoop
$E423 XorSpriteLoop2
$E43E XorSpriteSkip
$E452 XorSpriteSkip2
$E460 XorSpriteLoop3
$E47E XorSpriteLoop4
$E499 XorSpriteSkip3
$E4AD XorSpriteSkip4
> As DrawSprite $E318, but the screen address is in HL and the graphics in DE, and each byte is XORed into the screen: drawing twice removes the sprite. The last half byte of an odd width at an even column ($E442) never ran: no sprite of odd width is XORed there.
> Witness: L1 228035 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E4BB c XorBricksColoured ; XOR a brick graphic and colour its cells
> XorBricks $E4C4, then PaintAttrs $E2A3 with C. The only caller is the brick drawing at $D1A6.
> Witness: L1 204 (fire, l98, rzx x6).
$E4C4 c XorBricks ; XOR a CPC graphic onto the screen from its high nibbles
$E4E9 XorBricksLoop
$E4F9 XorBricksLoop2
$E514 XorBricksSkip
$E522 XorBricksSkip2
$E530 XorBricksLoop3
$E554 XorBricksLoop4
$E56F XorBricksSkip3
$E57D XorBricksSkip4
> As XorSprite $E3EE, but each Spectrum byte is (first byte & $F0) | (second byte >> 4): the other bit plane of the CPC graphic. The odd-column path $E530-$E58A never ran (the bricks lie at even columns).
> Witness: L1 204 (fire, l98, rzx x6).

@module hud ; The status line: score, lives, water, keys, hammers and level at the top of the screen
@about The status line is screen lines 1-7 (line-table entry 8): six fields printed with the font's digits and the icons 123-127 by PrintDigit and HudBlit, at the column in $AB18, which each character advances.
$E58B c DrawHud ; Redraw the whole status line
> Calls $B6EE, then ScoreToHud, LivesToHud, HammersToHud, WaterToHud, KeysToHud and LevelToHud. Called from the main loop ($ACB0, $ACC2) and the frame wait ($B6E6).
> Witness: L1 95989 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5A1 c ScoreToHud ; Print the score: five digits at column 3
> The score word $AAFB as five digits (PrintNumber5), columns 3-7.
> Witness: L1 96679 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5AD c LivesToHud ; Print the lives: icon 123 and two digits at column 9
> Icon character $7B at column 9, then the low byte of $AB01 as two digits (PrintNumber2).
> Witness: L1 96009 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5C0 c WaterToHud ; Print the water drops: icon 124 and two digits at column 14
> Icon $7C at column 14, then the byte $AAFD as two digits.
> Witness: L1 96110 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5D3 c KeysToHud ; Print the keys: icon 125 and two digits at column 18
> Icon $7D at column 18, then the byte $AAFE as two digits.
> Witness: L1 96372 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5E6 c HammersToHud ; Print the hammers: icon 126 and two digits at column 22
> Icon $7E at column 22, then the byte $AAFF as two digits.
> Witness: L1 96104 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E5F9 c LevelToHud ; Print the level: icon 127 and two digits at column 26
> Icon $7F at column 26, then Level $AB00 as two digits: the number shown is the level's index 0-99 as stored.
> Witness: L1 95991 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).
$E60C c PrintNumber5 ; Print HL as five decimal digits
