> Witness: L1 480586 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l x12, rzx00-rzx99).

@module text ; The text printer: control codes, single and double height, the font
@about PrintText prints a string of the font's characters 32-127 with control codes: $16 row col (AT), $01 single height, $02 double height, $10 n ink, $11 n paper, $12 n flash, $13 n bright, $14 n the whole attribute, $15 the default attribute again, $09 skip a column, $0D a new line, $00 the end. Other codes below 32 and above 127 print "?". The state is TextVars $E712-$E716.
$E667 c PrintText ; Print the string at HL with its control codes
$E695 PrintTextEnd
$E69C PrintTextAt
$E6A6 PrintTextSingle
$E6AC PrintTextDouble
$E6B3 PrintTextInk
$E6C3 PrintTextPaper
$E6D9 PrintTextFlash
$E6EC PrintTextBright
$E701 PrintTextAttr
$E709 PrintTextCode15
> Input: HL the string. Reads up to and including $00; at the end the attribute goes back to the default ($E712 to $E713) and HL points after the $00. $11 (paper) and $12 (flash) never ran. $12 and $13 cannot set their bit: the ROTATE through carry ($E6DB, $E6EE-$E6F0) puts the carry left by the CP (always clear) into the bit, so FLASH and BRIGHT are always cleared (known only from the code; the menu's "$13 1" at $F05A has no effect).
> Witness: L1 46858 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$E712 b TextVars ; The printer's state: default attribute, attribute, double height, row, column
$E713 TextAttr ; the attribute of the next characters
$E714 TextDouble ; 0 single height, 1 double height
$E715 TextRow ; the character row 0-23 (AT's first byte)
$E716 TextColumn ; the column 0-31 (AT's second byte)
$E717 c PrintChar ; Print character A, or act on $0D and $09
$E72D PrintCharSkip
$E732 PrintCharSkip2
$E737 PrintCharSkip3
> $0D: NewLine; $09: NextColumn; codes below 32 or from 128: "?"; else DrawGlyph. HL kept.
> Witness: L1 34443 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$E73E c DrawGlyph ; Draw font character A at TextRow, TextColumn in TextAttr, and move on a column
$E75F DrawGlyphLoop
$E786 DrawGlyphSkip
> The glyph Font + 8 x (A - 32) goes to the 8 lines of the character row (line-table entry 8 x row + 7, $B53B); at double height each row is drawn twice, so the character covers the row and the next. Then the attribute TextAttr at the cell (and the cell below at double height), and NextColumn.
> Witness: L1 34375 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$E7A9 c NextColumn ; TextColumn + 1; at 32 a new line
> Witness: L1 34443 (c1, fire, gameover, menu, twoname, l51, l59, l96, l98, rzx x24).
$E7B3 c NewLine ; TextColumn 0, TextRow + 1 (24 wraps to 0)
> Witness: L1 49 (menu, twoname).
$E7C6 c PrintOnceUponATime ; Print the text "Erase Una Vez ..." (never called)
> LD HL,$E7CD: CALL PrintText: RET. Nothing in the image calls it and no recording ran it (known only from the code). The text starts with the Amstrad CPC firmware's LOCATE code $1F row col, which PrintText does not know: it would print "?" for $1F and $0A. A leftover of the CPC original or of the Spanish conversion (the menu says "VERSIONED BY J250 <SPAIN>").
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$E7CD b OnceUponATimeText ; "Erase Una Vez ..." ("once upon a time", Spanish): CPC LOCATE 2,10, attribute $38, single then double height
@res texts/once-upon-a-time.bin
$E7E6 c XorLogo ; XOR the logo onto the menu screen (never called)
> XorSprite $E3EE with HL = $C066, DE = Logo, B = 60 lines, width 18: draws the logo of DrawLogo $E7F7 a second time, removing it. Not called and not run (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().

@module menu ; The menu: the logo, the options, the controls, demo and hall of fame in turn
@about Menu $EF33 prints the backdrop and the logo and waits: 0 starts the game, 1 toggles one and two players, 2-4 choose keyboard (Q A O P SPACE), Sinclair (9 8 6 7 0) or Kempston. After 3000 frames (60 s) without a key it returns for the demo or shows the hall of fame for 750 frames, alternately.
$E7F7 c DrawLogo ; Draw the BUMPY logo at the top of the menu screen
> DrawSprite $E318: Logo $E808, CPC address $C066 (character row 1, CPC byte 22: Spectrum line 1, column 11), width 18 CPC bytes (72 pixels), 60 lines. No attributes: the colours are the backdrop's (MenuBackdropText $F0E8).
> Witness: L1 8 (c1, gameover, menu, twoname).
$E808 b Logo 18 ; The BUMPY logo: 60 lines of 18 CPC mode-1 bytes (72 x 60 pixels)
@res sprites/logo.bin

@module hiscore ; Game over and the hall of fame: the place, the name entry, the table screen
@about The hall of fame is four PrintText strings of 32 bytes (HallOfFame $EEB3): AT, attribute, double height, a three-letter name, " : ", five digits and the score word. A player whose score is not below the lowest entry ($EF25) enters it when his game ends (GameOver $B755, EnterHallOfFame $EC40).
$EC40 c EnterHallOfFame ; Game over: flash the border, put the score in the hall of fame and enter the name
$EC42 EnterHallOfFameLoop
$EC45 EnterHallOfFameLoop2
$EC64 EnterHallOfFameLoop3
$EC8E EnterHallOfFameSkip
$EC9D EnterHallOfFameSkip2
> Called by the game over $B777. 250 x 8 border colours 7..0 with a delay ($B873, B = 2) between them. Then the place: the score $AAFB is compared with the scores (+$12) of records 1-3 only; at the first lower one, the records from there down to the third move one down (LDDR $EF12 to $EF32, $60 - 32 x place bytes) and the score goes in its place. A score below all three goes to record 4 without a compare (GameOver called this routine only because the score is not below record 4's, $EF25). In the gameover recording the score went to record 4 and the move at $EC8E never ran.
> The record gets the score, the name "..." ($2E), its digits (ScoreToDigits); then ShowHallOfFame and EnterName. Output: ($ED93) = the record.
