@module sound ; The sound: beeper effects, their primitives, and the unused 128K and interrupt code
@about Every effect is LD HL,params: CALL primitive(s): RET, followed by 16 bytes of parameters. No primitive reads them (the CPC original's envelopes, known only from the code): they are kept as leftovers. The primitives drive the speaker (bit 4 of port $FE) with interrupts off: sweeps, random noise from R, and noise from ROM bytes.
@about $F183-$F3C7 holds code no recording ran and nothing calls: a random number, an AY register writer for the 128K, a delay, a line-table builder, and two IM 2 set-ups whose handlers call empty hooks. It is the frame of a 128K music driver that this release does not have.
$F183 c Random ; A = a random byte from R (never called)
> R twice XORed into an address in HL, then R XOR (that address). HL kept. Nothing calls it (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$F190 c WriteAyRegisters ; Write AY registers 0-13 from the 14 bytes at HL (never called)
$F192 WriteAyRegistersLoop
> For each register A = 0-13: C = (HL), WriteAyRegister. Nothing calls it (known only from the code).
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$F19F c WriteAyRegister ; Write C to AY register A (128K ports $FFFD, $BFFD)
$F1A9 WriteAyRegisterSkip
> Register 7 (the mixer) is masked with $3F, so the I/O ports stay inputs. OUT ($FFFD),A then OUT ($BFFD),C, interrupts off between. Known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$F1B8 b UnusedF1B8 ; 16 bytes: seven NOPs, $FF and eight NOPs
$F1C8 c DelayAfterFrame ; HALT, then a loop of 512 (never called)
$F1CE DelayAfterFrameLoop
> Known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$F1D5 c BuildLineTable ; Write the 192 Spectrum line addresses at $F206 (never called)
$F1DB BuildLineTableLoop
> For line 0-191: $4000 + (line & $C0) x 32 + (line & 7) x 256 + (line & $38) x 4, stored as words from $F206: the table $B52D holds the same addresses ready made. Run, it would write $F206-$F385 over this code and the IM 2 set-ups (known only from the code).
